          text="Wiedrich's battery stood on a hill north of the Baltimore turnpike near the cemetery and east of the town (Hunt: in front of the cemetery entrance). During the afternoon it skirmished with a rebel battery posted near the York road. About 5 p.m., when the Union infantry that had advanced through the town was repulsed, Wiedrich changed the direction of his pieces, opened with shells on the columns advancing from the town, and then fired canister at the enemy that appeared in front; the fire was kept up until late in the evening. Howard and Hunt place the enemy's single attempt to turn the right at about 4.30 p.m. and say Wiedrich's battery broke the line. Nothing in the reports ties this fire to the railroad cut (the cut lies on Oak Ridge, far from his front).",
