          when="Night of Jul 1 to the small hours of Jul 2 (Ewell: Johnson's reply after midnight)",
          basis="Grover OR27-1-R038 pp.284-285; Ewell OR27-2-R467 pp.445-446; Johnson OR27-2-R481 p.504. Window limits: the start 19:30 is sunset (computed); the end 04:00 is the hour before the day was 'now breaking' (Ewell) in LMT. A limit is an allowed bound, not a claim of when the event happened; the reader adds its own clock tolerance at a clock-time limit.",
          text="Grover (7th Indiana) reports that on the afternoon of Jul 1, by Wadsworth's order, the regiment took position on the hill east of Gettysburg, then the extreme right of the Union line, and began a temporary breastwork; during the following night a force of the enemy attempted to penetrate the line but was easily driven off, supposing itself confronted by a heavy force; on the night of the 1st Sergeant Hussey, unassisted, captured a lieutenant of the 25th Virginia and drove off a squad of 20 men. Ewell reports that Johnson, after forming his line on this side of the wooded hill, sent a reconnoitering party to it; near the summit it was met by a superior force that captured part of it while the rest escaped; Johnson therefore refrained from taking the hill and, at daybreak, it was too late for a change. The tie between the two accounts is an inference; the seed's 'and man' (a second prisoner) is not in Grover's report.",
