          text="Hancock says Wadsworth's division and a battery were placed on the eminence just across the turnpike, which the division held to the end of the operations, and that he sent Geary's division to the high ground near Round Top. Doubleday records that Hancock directed him to send a force to support a battery on a lower range of hills about 100 yards east of the cemetery position and that he sent the rest of Wadsworth's division there, after which orders from Howard, who ranked Hancock, to send the troops in another direction caused some delay and confusion. Howard says Hancock was to arrange the troops and place the batteries on the left of the Baltimore pike while he took the right, and that Wadsworth's division was sent to a height to the right and rear. Wadsworth: on the evening of the 1st he was ordered to occupy a hill on the right of the cemetery. Hancock's exact words to Wadsworth are not in any report. The seed's 'Little Round Top' is Hancock's 'Round Top Mountain' (Geary, T1-176).",
